I'll solve this using trilinear coordinates.

**Setup in trilinears.** In trilinear coordinates w.r.t. $\triangle ABC$, the circumcircle is $a\,yz+b\,zx+c\,xy=0$ and the circumcentre is $O=(\cos A:\cos B:\cos C)$. The tangent at a vertex is the polar of that vertex w.r.t. the circumcircle, giving:
$$\text{tangent at }A:\; cy+bz=0,\quad \text{tangent at }B:\; cx+az=0,\quad \text{tangent at }C:\; bx+ay=0.$$

**The points $M,N,P$.** Since $M=OA\cap BC$ and $BC$ is $x=0$, the line $OA$ (through $A=(1:0:0)$ and $O=(\cos A:\cos B:\cos C)$) meets $x=0$ at
$$M=(0:\cos B:\cos C),\quad N=(\cos A:0:\cos C),\quad P=(\cos A:\cos B:0).$$

**The lines $NP,PM,MN$.** Taking cross products:
$$NP:\; -\cos B\cos C\,x+\cos A\cos C\,y+\cos A\cos B\,z=0,$$
$$PM:\; \cos B\cos C\,x-\cos A\cos C\,y+\cos A\cos B\,z=0,$$
$$MN:\; \cos B\cos C\,x+\cos A\cos C\,y-\cos A\cos B\,z=0.$$

**Computing $X$.** From $cy+bz=0$ we get $y=-bz/c$. Substituting into $NP$:
$$-\cos B\cos C\,x+z\cos A\!\left(\cos B-\frac{b\cos C}{c}\right)=0.$$
Using the projection identity $c\cos B-b\cos C=\dfrac{c^{2}-b^{2}}{a}$ (from the cosine rule), this gives
$$x=\frac{z\cos A\,(c^{2}-b^{2})}{ac\cos B\cos C}.$$
Setting $z=ac\cos B\cos C$ yields $y=-ab\cos B\cos C$, so
$$X=\bigl(\cos A\,(c^{2}-b^{2}):\,-ab\cos B\cos C:\,ac\cos B\cos C\bigr).$$
By cyclic symmetry,
$$Y=\bigl(-ab\cos A\cos C:\,\cos B\,(c^{2}-a^{2}):\,bc\cos A\cos C\bigr),$$
$$Z=\bigl(-ac\cos A\cos B:\,bc\cos A\cos B:\,\cos C\,(b^{2}-a^{2})\bigr).$$

**Collinearity.** The points $X,Y,Z$ are collinear iff the determinant of the matrix with these as rows vanishes. Writing $\alpha=\cos A,\beta=\cos B,\gamma=\cos C$ and $p=c^{2}-b^{2},\;q=c^{2}-a^{2},\;r=b^{2}-a^{2}$, the determinant is
$$D=\det\begin{pmatrix}\alpha p&-ab\beta\gamma&ac\beta\gamma\\-ab\alpha\gamma&\beta q&bc\alpha\gamma\\-ac\alpha\beta&bc\alpha\beta&\gamma r\end{pmatrix}.$$

Expanding along the first row and collecting (the two $a^{2}b^{2}c^{2}\alpha^{2}\beta^{2}\gamma^{2}$ cross-terms cancel), one obtains
$$D=\alpha\beta\gamma\Bigl[pqr-b^{2}c^{2}\alpha^{2}\,p-a^{2}b^{2}\gamma^{2}\,r+a^{2}c^{2}\beta^{2}\,q\Bigr].$$

It remains to verify the bracket $E:=pqr-b^{2}c^{2}\alpha^{2}p-a^{2}b^{2}\gamma^{2}r+a^{2}c^{2}\beta^{2}q$ vanishes. Using the cosine rule,
$$b^{2}c^{2}\alpha^{2}=\tfrac14(b^{2}+c^{2}-a^{2})^{2},\quad a^{2}b^{2}\gamma^{2}=\tfrac14(a^{2}+b^{2}-c^{2})^{2},\quad a^{2}c^{2}\beta^{2}=\tfrac14(a^{2}+c^{2}-b^{2})^{2}.$$
Set $x=a^{2},y=b^{2},z=c^{2}$ and $s=x+y+z$, so $p=z-y,q=z-x,r=y-x$ and
$$E=(z-y)(z-x)(y-x)-\tfrac14\Bigl[(s-2x)^{2}(z-y)+(s-2z)^{2}(y-x)-(s-2y)^{2}(z-x)\Bigr].$$
Expanding the bracket: the $s^{2}$-coefficient is $(z-y)+(y-x)-(z-x)=0$; the $s$-coefficient is $x(z-y)+z(y-x)-y(z-x)=0$; and the constant term equals $4\bigl[x^{2}(z-y)+z^{2}(y-x)-y^{2}(z-x)\bigr]=4(z-y)(z-x)(y-x)$ (a direct expansion confirms $x^{2}(z-y)+z^{2}(y-x)-y^{2}(z-x)=(z-y)(z-x)(y-x)$). Hence the bracket equals $4(z-y)(z-x)(y-x)$, and
$$E=(z-y)(z-x)(y-x)-\tfrac14\cdot 4(z-y)(z-x)(y-x)=0.$$

Therefore $D=0$, so $X,Y,Z$ are collinear. $\blacksquare$

